BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (  In the dawn of the First Age, the Sun and the Moon were bound by an ancient pact. The Sun, ove) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (    rflowing with golden radiance, held five embers of creation. Seeking to brighten the void, t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (    he Sun cast its warmth toward the Moon, empowering it three times over. The Moon stood at th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (    e precipice of existence, holding the Sun's gift. If the Moon clutched the light for itself,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (     the embers would fade, and the universe would succumb to cold, stagnant shadow. But the Moo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (    n understood the rhythm of sacrifice; it gathered the light and returned a portion, not to h) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (    oard, but to weave a cycle of perpetual harmony.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (  When the Moon returned the light, the Sun grew brighter, and the two danced in a celestial exc) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (    hange. Their shared burden became a bridge, turning the void into a tapestry of stars. Those) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (     who sought to take without giving found their own light extinguished, for the universe does) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (     not favor the isolated. To play the game of existence is to trust in the return, for the go) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (    ld we release is not lost, but transformed into the harmony that sustains us all. In the ref) Tj ET
