BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (      In the dawn of time, the Titan of Sprouting, Aethel, held five seeds of golden wheat. He s) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (        tood before the Weaver of Petals, Elara, who tended the barren fields of the firmament. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (        Aethel knew that if he buried his seeds in the earth, they would vanish from his grasp, ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (        but he understood the secret of the soil: it possessed the power to magnify his gift thr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (        eefold. Yet, the soil was silent and autonomous; it could choose to hoard the surplus or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (         return the promise of future harvests.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (      Aethel extended his palm, offering the golden grains to the Weaver. He did not fear the vo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (        id, for trust is the loom upon which reality is woven. Elara, seeing the sacrifice, did ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (        not bury the grain in the darkness of greed. She understood the great cycle-that for the) Tj ET
